@@s001 Max-flow min-cut: the minimum cut
@@s002 ok:reachable from s in the residual network,no:arc of the cut (capacity = value of the flow),dato:side t
@@s003 Maximum flow in the previous network. I compute the residual network and the set <b>A</b> of the nodes reachable from s.
@@s004 From s: s → a is saturated, s → b is saturated. No residual arc leaves s: <b>A = {s}</b>.
@@s005 The cut (A, B) has capacity: arcs from A towards B, that is s → a and s → b: <b>1 + 1 = 2</b>. It is equal to the maximum flow: max-flow = min-cut.
@@s006 Max-flow min-cut: the minimum cut
@@s007 The max-flow min-cut theorem says that the value of the maximum flow equals the capacity of the minimum cut. The cut is read from the final residual network, and with disjoint paths it gives Menger's theorem.
@@s008 The cut
@@s009 A <b>cut</b> <code>(A, B)</code> divides the nodes into two sets, with <code>s ∈ A</code> and <code>t ∈ B</code>. Its <b>capacity</b> is the sum of the capacities of the arcs going from <code>A</code> to <code>B</code>. Every flow crosses the cut: so the value of the flow is at most the capacity of the cut.
@@s010 The cut from the final residual network
@@s011 The theorem
@@s012 <b>Max-flow min-cut</b>: value of the maximum flow = capacity of the minimum cut. The cut is found at the end: <code>A</code> is the set of nodes reachable from <code>s</code> in the final residual network. The arcs leaving <code>A</code> are the cut.
@@s013 Menger: disjoint paths
@@s014 <b>Menger</b>: the maximum number of <b>arc-disjoint</b> <code>s–t</code> paths equals the minimum number of arcs to remove to detach <code>t</code> from <code>s</code>. For nodes (node-disjoint paths, or nodes to remove) the node-split gadget is used (see the gadgets page).
@@s015 Project selection: choosing with the cut
@@s016 You have projects, each with a <b>profit</b> <code>pᵢ</code>. The profit can be negative: it is a cost. Some projects <b>require</b> others: if you take <i>i</i>, you must also take <i>j</i>. You want the feasible set of maximum profit.
@@s017 The trick: the minimum cut decides on which side each project lies. I build the network:
@@s018 <code>s → i</code> with capacity <code>pᵢ</code>, if <code>pᵢ &gt; 0</code>. If I leave the project out of the side of <code>s</code>, I pay <code>pᵢ</code>.
@@s019 <code>i → t</code> with capacity <code>−pᵢ</code>, if <code>pᵢ &lt; 0</code>. If I take it (on the side of <code>s</code>), I pay the cost.
@@s020 <code>i → j</code> with capacity <code>∞</code>, if <i>i</i> requires <i>j</i>. <code>∞</code> means "not cuttable": a finite cut cannot have <i>i</i> on the side of <code>s</code> and <i>j</i> on the other, so the requirements are respected.
@@s021 Let <code>C</code> be the sum of the positive profits. For a cut <code>(A, B)</code> with <code>s</code> in <code>A</code>, I take the projects of <code>A</code>. The cost of the cut is <code>C − profit(A)</code>: I pay the positive profits left out and the costs of those taken. So:
@@s022 <code>maximum profit = C − minimum cut</code>
@@s023 Project selection: the minimum cut says which projects to take
@@s024 The Kattis problem <i>Open-Pit Mining</i> is exactly this: each block of rock is a project with a profit (value of the ore minus the digging cost), and to dig a block you must have dug those that cover it. The flow code is that of <i>Ford-Fulkerson</i>, with the matrix of residuals.
@@s025 Other models of chapter 7. Each row is a pattern: the work is recognising the shape in the text.
