@@s034 circulation with demands
@@s035 node <i>v</i> with demand <code>dᵥ</code>: super-source <code>s*</code> → those who produce (capacity −dᵥ), those who consume → super-sink <code>t*</code> (capacity dᵥ). A circulation exists if the maximum flow saturates all the arcs of <code>s*</code>
@@s036 lower bounds <code>ℓₑ ≤ f(e)</code>
@@s037 I immediately move <code>ℓₑ</code> on each arc, adjust the demands and look for a circulation with capacity <code>cₑ − ℓₑ</code>
@@s038 survey design
@@s039 customers and products as in matching, with lower and upper limits on how many questions per customer and per product
@@s040 airline scheduling
@@s041 flights as arcs with lower limit 1 (they must be covered); the planes are the flow
@@s042 image segmentation
@@s043 minimum cut: each pixel is "foreground" or "background"; the arcs pay the wrong probability and the separation between neighbouring pixels
@@s044 project selection
@@s045 see above: positive profits from <code>s</code>, costs towards <code>t</code>, requirements with <code>∞</code>
@@s046 baseball elimination
@@s047 the remaining games are nodes that distribute wins to the teams; the team is eliminated if the flow does not saturate
